From: manishbazz <manishbazz@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 11:28:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add WebGL2 GPU backend for the flow simulation, CPU as
 automatic fallback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- Split into src/components/panels/flow/: types.ts, supportsGpuFlowSim.ts,
  createCpuFlowSim.ts (extracted, unchanged logic from the working
  version), createGpuFlowSim.ts (new)
- GPU backend runs the same pipeline (boundary enforcement, divergence,
  Jacobi pressure iteration, semi-Lagrangian advection for both velocity
  and dye) as fragment shader passes over RG16F/R16F ping-pong textures
  instead of JS for-loops over Float32Arrays — same physics, same
  constants (BASE_TIME_SCALE, PROJECT_ITERATIONS, stripe params, etc),
  parallelized on the GPU
- Feature detection (supportsGpuFlowSim) runs on a disposable throwaway
  canvas before ever touching the real one — calling getContext('webgl2')
  permanently locks a canvas out of ever getting a 2D context afterward,
  so detection must not risk that on the canvas actually used
- FlowSimulation.tsx now just picks a backend and owns the UI (play/pause
  button, canvas element) — identical markup/behavior regardless of
  which backend is running underneath

Caveat: I don't have a real browser/GPU in my sandbox to visually verify
WebGL rendering output — only tsc/build correctness is confirmed here.
Please test locally and report back exactly what you see (or any
console errors) so I can fix anything that looks wrong.
